===Postwar===
After the war, Keynes continued to represent the United Kingdom in international negotiations despite his deteriorating health. He succeeded in obtaining preferential terms from the United States [[Anglo-American loan|for new and outstanding debts]] to facilitate the rebuilding of the British economy.{{r|timeline|at=[https://www.maynardkeynes.org/keynes-career-timeline.html#intfin §1945 to 1946]}}

Just before his death in 1946, Keynes told Henry Clay, a professor of social economics and advisor to the [[Bank of England]],<ref>{{cite book
|first=Richard
|last=Sayers
|authorlink=Richard Sidney Sayers
|title=The Bank of England, 1891–1944|volume=1
|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=8hY9AAAAIAAJ&pg=PA325
|isbn=978-0521210676
|year=1976
|publisher=CUP Archive
