===Death===
[[File:Tilton House.jpg|thumb|Tilton House, 2021]]

Throughout his life, Keynes worked energetically for the benefit both of the public and his friends; even when his health was poor, he laboured to sort out the finances of his old college.<ref name="JMK Ind08Nov08">{{cite news |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/business/analysis-and-features/john-maynard-keynes-can-the-great-economist-save-the-world-994416.html |title=John Maynard Keynes: Can the great economist save the world? |access-date=20 November 2008 |work=The Independent |location=United Kingdom |first=Nick |last=Fraser |date=8 November 2008 |archive-date=28 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200728201442/https://www.independent.co.uk/news/business/analysis-and-features/john-maynard-keynes-can-the-great-economist-save-the-world-994416.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Helping to set up the [[Bretton Woods system]], he worked to institute an [[international monetary system]] that would be beneficial for the [[world economy]]. In 1946, Keynes suffered a series of heart attacks, which ultimately proved fatal. They began during negotiations for the [[Anglo-American loan]] in [[Savannah, Georgia]], where he was trying to secure favourable terms for the United Kingdom from the United States, a process he described as "absolute hell".<ref name="50 greats">{{cite book |last=Pressman |first=Steven |authorlink=Steven Pressman (economist) |title=Fifty Major Economists |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=8_mEAgAAQBAJ&pg=PA99 |year=1999 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1134780822 |pages=99–104 |access-date=21 April 2017 |archive-date=15 June 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200615010012/https://books.google.com/books?id=8_mEAgAAQBAJ&pg=PA99 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite book |last=Marr |first=Andrew |authorlink=Andrew Marr |title=A history of modern Britain |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=CF9nAAAAMAAJ&pg=PP1 |year=2007 |publisher=Macmillan |location=London |isbn=978-1405005388 |page=12 |access-date=21 April 2017 |archive-date=15 June 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200615001313/https://books.google.com/books?id=CF9nAAAAMAAJ&pg=PP1 |url-status=live }}</ref> A few weeks after returning from the United States, Keynes died of a heart attack at Tilton, his farmhouse home near [[Firle]], [[East Sussex]], England, on 21 April 1946, at the age of 62.{{r|2003 Skidel|p=[https://archive.org/details/johnmaynardkeyne0000skid_q4f4/page/832/mode/2up 832]}}<ref>{{cite news |title=Lord Keynes Dies of Heart Attack. Noted Economist Exhausted by Strain of Recent Savannah Monetary Conference |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1946/04/22/archives/lord-keynes-dies-of-heart-attack-noted-economist-exhausted-by.html|work=The New York Times |date=22 April 1946 |access-date=10 February 2010 |archive-date=17 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210417195235/https://www.nytimes.com/1946/04/22/archives/lord-keynes-dies-of-heart-attack-noted-economist-exhausted-by.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Against his wishes (he wanted his ashes to be deposited in the crypt at King's), his ashes were scattered on the Downs above Tilton.<ref>Wilson, Scott. ''Resting Places: The Burial Sites of More Than 14,000 Famous Persons'', 3d ed.: 2 (Kindle Location 25430). McFarland & Company, Inc., Publishers. Kindle Edition.</ref>

Both of Keynes's parents outlived him: his father [[John Neville Keynes]] (1852–1949) by three years, and his mother [[Florence Ada Keynes]] (1861–1958) by twelve. Keynes's brother Sir [[Geoffrey Keynes]] (1887–1982) was a distinguished surgeon, scholar and [[bibliophile]]. Keynes's sister, [[Margaret Hill (social reformer)|Margaret Hill]] (1885–1970) was a prominent social reformer. His nephews include [[Richard Keynes]] (1919–2010), a physiologist, and [[Quentin Keynes]] (1921–2003), an adventurer and bibliophile. His niece, [[Polly Hill (economist)|Polly Hill]] (1914–2005), was an economic anthropologist and emeritus fellow of [[Clare Hall, Cambridge]].

